  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nCurrent satellite water vapor imagery shows a compact and robust PV \nwave over eastern Colorado moving eastward. This system has sparked \noff numerous showers with some embedded thunderstorms over southwest \nKansas due to modest 700mb warm advection and weak elevated \ninstability. RAP and REFS short range models depict this axis of \nmodest lift and weak elevated instability to spread into southern \nKansas this morning. Therefore, expecting scattered to numerous \nshowers to spread across southern Kansas during the morning and\nafternoon hours. Can't rule out a few short-lived embedded \nstorms near the KS/OK border this morning. This activity will \ngradually shift east of the area by this evening. Upper level \nridging briefly builds across the central plains on Friday with \nsurface high pressure settling over Kansas and temperatures \nslightly below normal. \n\nModels continue to show a weak upper trough racing southeast across \nthe region for Saturday. Not expecting any precipitation since the \nweak system will have virtually no moisture to work with. Meanwhile, \nlong range deterministic models and model ensembles are in solid \nagreement with a high amplitude upper ridge building across the \nRockies. This creates high confidence for above normal temperatures \nand a dry weather pattern to prevail for Sunday through all of next \nweek.",
